    "narrative": "The offensive ceiling for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who commands an elite score of 75.83. Despite the challenge of an elite opposing catcher potentially hindering his base-stealing opportunities, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s placement in the #2 hole against Landen Roupp provides the best combination of lineup protection and high-leverage scoring potential. Alongside him, **Josh Naylor** (65.29) and **Austin Riley** (64.31) emerge as core plays. **Austin Riley** is the beneficiary of a pristine matchup against a soft starter with a 9.71 xERA, while **Josh Naylor** finds himself in an advantageous spot against Anthony Kay. **Mickey Moniak** remains a sneaky strong start (60.24) as well; while the platoon split against a lefty traditionally hurts, the extreme park factor boost for tonight outweighs those concerns.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who finished just outside the starting rotation with a score of 41.15. While **Vinnie Pasquantino** is a reliable bat, he fell victim to a crowded field of high-efficiency options and a poor wind-aided environment, as well as a disadvantageous platoon split against Suarez. We must also exercise caution with **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Gabriel Moreno**, as their scores are currently based on pending lineups; should these players slide down the order, their efficiency scores will likely regress further. Furthermore, while **Brice Turang** holds a respectable 55.48, we are banking on his aggressive base-stealing potential against a pitcher who struggles to control the run game. We continue to monitor the status of our sidelined core, specifically **Jose Caballero**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, and **Trevor Story**, who remain on the IL, while depth pieces like **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Alex Freeland**, **Kyle Karros**, and **Carson Williams** remain in reserve as insurance.",
